arXiv:2606.13815v1 [cs.AI] 11 Jun 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\optauthor</span>
<br class="ltx_break">
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p">Indian Institute of Technology Roorkee
 and  
<br class="ltx_break">Indian Institute of Technology Roorkee
 and  
<br class="ltx_break">Raeth AI</p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Pratham Singla
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:pratham_s@me.iitr.ac.in">pratham_s@me.iitr.ac.in</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shivank Garg
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:shivank_g@mfs.iitr.ac.in">shivank_g@mfs.iitr.ac.in</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vihan Singh
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:vihan@raeth.ai">vihan@raeth.ai</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Strategic reasoning under uncertainty underpins consequential decisions in negotiation, finance, and policy, but prevailing game-play benchmarks collapse heterogeneous reasoning dimensions into a single scalar, leaving the capability structure of frontier LLMs unexamined. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Poker Arena</span>, a no-limit Texas Hold’em tournament platform that couples a three-layer memory architecture (within-hand, session, and cross-session) with a nine-axis cognitive profile decomposing strategic reasoning into interpretable dimensions such as bet-sizing calibration and positional awareness. We evaluate seven frontier models across 50 sessions of 1,000 hands and a controlled memory ablation; tournament chips and aggregate axis score order the field differently: Claude Opus 4.6 wins <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\$15{,}730" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>15,730</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\$15{,}730</annotation></semantics></math> chips with 14 first-place finishes, yet ranks only fifth of seven on mean axis score, while persistent memory helps some models and hurts others. These findings show that multi-axis evaluation surfaces capability structure that scalar leaderboards systematically misrank, with cross-dimensional consistency outweighing peak performance on any single axis.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Frontier large language models (LLMs) are increasingly expected to plan and act under partial observability against adaptive opponents, conditions that put strategic reasoning under uncertainty at the centre of contemporary LLM evaluation. Poker is a particularly dense instantiation: within a single decision an agent must size a bet, decide whether to bluff, model heterogeneous opponents, and reason about expected value under stochastic run-outs. These competencies are dissociable, which makes poker a natural testbed for decomposing strategic reasoning rather than reducing it to a single outcome.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two challenges set the evaluation of frozen-weight LLMs in poker apart from classical poker AI. State-of-the-art poker agents rely on counterfactual regret minimization and large-scale self-play search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bowling et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown and Sandholm, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown and Sandholm, 2019</a>)</cite>, which are training-time methods inapplicable at inference; conversely, naive prompt-based agents lack persistence across hands, discarding the historical signal strategic adaptation depends on. Existing memory architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Packer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite> have been studied primarily in single-agent or cooperative settings, leaving competitive multi-agent behaviour largely uncharacterised, while game-play benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta Fundamental AI Research Diplomacy Team (FAIR) et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> report aggregate metrics that conflate distinct strategic competencies and rarely treat persistent memory as a controlled variable. A full survey of related work (LLM game-play benchmarks, classical poker AI, opponent modelling, persistent-memory architectures, and theory-of-mind evaluations) is in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Related Work ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13815v1/fig1_chip_growth.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/212;" width="405" height="212" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cumulative chip totals across 1,000 hands (50 sessions <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 20 hands, seven seats per session, $1,000 buy-in). Claude finishes at $16,730, roughly four times Grok’s $4,705 second-place margin; GPT, DeepSeek, Gemini, and Qwen settle within a $2,000 band of the buy-in line; Kimi trails at <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\$11{,}558" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" mathsize="1.110em" rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>11,558</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\$11{,}558</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We address these gaps with Poker Arena, an end-to-end no-limit Texas Hold’em tournament platform pairing a controllable three-layer memory system (within-hand context, model-controlled session notebook, and cross-session lifetime store) with a nine-axis profile of strategic competence spanning bet sizing, bluffing, opponent reading, composure, adaptability, prediction, strategic mixing, factual accuracy, and positional awareness. Across 50 seven-player tournaments comprising 1,000 hands and a controlled memory ablation (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the tournament winner ranks fifth on mean axis score and persistent memory improves some models while degrading others, both showing that consistent cross-axis competence and model-specific memory interfaces matter more than scalar leaderboard rank.
We make the following major contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Poker Arena platform.</span> An end-to-end no-limit Texas Hold’em tournament environment that logs actions, reasoning, and memory updates across seven frontier LLMs, with deterministic side-pot, timeout, and showdown handling.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Three-layer memory architecture.</span> A within-hand, session, and lifetime memory system in which Layer 2 is written by the agent itself and Layer 3 re-seeds subsequent sessions, enabling targeted ablations over the temporal scale of persistence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nine-axis cognitive profile and empirical study.</span> A profile that decomposes strategic reasoning into nine interpretable axes spanning deterministic and judge-scored measures, paired with a 1,000-hand evaluation across seven frontier models showing that scalar leaderboards misrank competence and that memory effects are model-dependent.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Poker Arena Framework</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Poker Arena evaluates LLM agents in no-limit Texas Hold’em tournaments and records actions, reasoning traces, and memory updates. The framework comprises four components: a game engine, a three-layer memory system, a prompt pipeline, and a model gateway over seven frontier models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Game Engine</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each session comprises twenty hands seated with up to seven agents, with all agents beginning at $1,000 in chips. Blinds escalate across four tiers every five hands ($5/$10, $10/$20, $25/$50, $50/$100) to introduce tournament pressure as effective stack depth shortens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Hand rankings are evaluated by a deterministic five-from-seven solver, and win probabilities at each decision point are estimated via Monte Carlo run-out sampling. Side pots are tracked through per-player contribution accounting, ensuring correct payoff allocation under unequal stack sizes and preserving fidelity to standard no-limit tournament rules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Three-Layer Memory</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Strategic reasoning in poker spans multiple temporal scales, including within-hand inference, across-hand adaptation, and cross-session learning. To capture these dynamics, we organise memory into three layers (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Layer 3: Cross-session memory. ‣ 2.2 Three-Layer Memory ‣ 2 Poker Arena Framework ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> denote agents instantiated by models <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> index hands within session <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\{\text{pre},\text{flop},\text{turn},\text{river}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>pre</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>flop</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>turn</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>river</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\{\text{pre},\text{flop},\text{turn},\text{river}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote betting rounds. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{i}^{(h)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{i}^{(h)}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the private hole cards of agent <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{(h,s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{(h,s)}</annotation></semantics></math> the community cards, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{(h,s)}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{(h,s)}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> the action space, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{\text{fold}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>fold</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{\text{fold}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">check</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">call</span>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{bet}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>bet</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{bet}(b)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{raise}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>raise</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{raise}(b)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{all-in}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>all-in</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{all-in}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layer 1: Within-hand context.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Layer 1 defines an ephemeral decision context,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}^{(h,s)}=\phi\big(\mathcal{H}_{i}^{(h)},\,\mathcal{C}^{(h,s)},\,\text{history}^{(h,s)},\,\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>history</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}^{(h,s)}=\phi\big(\mathcal{H}_{i}^{(h)},\,\mathcal{C}^{(h,s)},\,\text{history}^{(h,s)},\,\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is reconstructed at each decision point and discarded immediately afterward. The transformation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> anonymizes opponents using session-stable aliases, preventing model-specific biases in opponent modeling. It further integrates the betting history with the agent’s prior reasoning traces, ensuring the within-hand context self-contained at every decision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layer 2: Session-level memory.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Layer 2 is a session-scoped memory <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, represented as a single text buffer per agent and session, with a maximum size of 16K characters. This memory is written and maintained by the agent itself. After each hand, the agent is provided with an anonymized summary <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(h_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(h_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and performs a conditional update:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}\leftarrow\begin{cases}\operatorname{LLM}_{i}\big(\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)},\,\sigma(h_{t})\big)&amp;\text{if }\operatorname{LLM}_{i}\text{ emits {UPDATE:yes}}\\
\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>LLM</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mtext> emits </mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">UPDATE:yes</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}\leftarrow\begin{cases}\operatorname{LLM}_{i}\big(\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)},\,\sigma(h_{t})\big)&amp;\text{if }\operatorname{LLM}_{i}\text{ emits {UPDATE:yes}}\\
\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This design allows agents to selectively retain and refine strategic information across hands, rather than accumulating unfiltered experience.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layer 3: Cross-session memory.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Layer 3 is a persistent lifetime memory <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that survives across sessions. At the end of each session, a consolidation function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> updates <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from the final session memory <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. At the start of the next session, a seeding policy <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> initializes the session memory as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k+1)}\leftarrow\pi(\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)},\mathcal{L}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k+1)}\leftarrow\pi(\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)},\mathcal{L}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, preferring <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as fallback.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider three configurations of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>: <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">normal</span>, which propagates the prior-session memory forward; <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">past</span>, which loads an external offline memory file (Ablation A); and <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fresh</span>, which initializes an empty memory (Ablation B).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13815v1/figures/full_architecture.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/239;" width="405" height="239" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Poker Arena architecture: a three-layer memory hierarchy (Layer 1 within-hand context, Layer 2 session notebook, Layer 3 cross-session lifetime store) feeding seven frontier LLMs over a deterministic Texas Hold’em engine, scored on a nine-axis cognitive profile.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Prompting and model integration are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Prompt Templates and Model Integration ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Cognitive Profile</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Poker Arena reports a nine-axis cognitive profile spanning deterministic, regex-verified, and judge-scored measures (Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Cognitive Profile ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Five axes use deterministic action-log statistics, two use regex matches against ground truth in reasoning text, and two are hybrids that pair deterministic counts with an LLM judge. Each axis returns a score in <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>; we report both the per-axis values and their unweighted mean <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{M}=\tfrac{1}{9}\sum_{k=1}^{9}M_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>9</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{M}=\tfrac{1}{9}\sum_{k=1}^{9}M_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The nine axes of the Poker Arena cognitive profile. <span id="S3.T1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Source</span>: <span id="S3.T1.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Det</span> = computed from the action log alone; <span id="S3.T1.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Regex</span> = pattern-match against reasoning text; <span id="S3.T1.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hybrid (NJ)</span> = deterministic counts plus an <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-judge LLM panel.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.9" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.9.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.9.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.2pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">ID</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.9.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.2pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.9.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Axis</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.9.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.2pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.9.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Source</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.9.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.2pt 3.0pt;">
