<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Discussion and Limitations</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The chip ranking and the mean-axis ranking disagree by design rather than by accident. Chip P&amp;L compounds local edges into a wealth process whose variance scales with bet sizing and risk-taking, so a single high-aggression model can dominate the chip leaderboard without dominating the mean axis. The mean axis, by contrast, weights every dimension equally and credits competence whether or not it earns chips this session. Claude’s profile is a tight 0.143-SD spread across nine axes; Grok’s profile is taller in three places and shorter in others. The 1,000-hand horizon rewards the first shape because a recurring weakness is a recurring leak, while a single specialty cannot compensate when the table is varied (annotated hand traces illustrating each pattern are in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Annotated Hand Traces ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>). This is the same disaggregation argument HELM made for general capability evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; a multi-axis profile is not a substitute for the leaderboard, it is the object the leaderboard summarises.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Persistent memory shows the same model-specificity. The three ablated models map onto a clean Bayesian-update reading: GPT treats the seeded summary as a useful prior and converts it into chips, Kimi over-weights the prior and under-reacts to live evidence, and Claude is roughly invariant at this scale. Empirically this matches what the explicit-Bayesian opponent-modelling literature found in classical poker AI: exploitation gain is a function of how an agent integrates priors with online evidence, not of whether the priors are present <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Southey et al., 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganzfried and Sandholm, 2011</a>)</cite>. The implication for benchmarking is concrete; a fair comparison requires the (model, memory-interface) cross-product, not a single fixed memory configuration.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations.</span> Four caveats bound these conclusions. The 1,000-hand main panel and 100-hand-per-cell ablation are small relative to the variance of no-limit poker, leaving the middle of the leaderboard a statistical tie and the ablation <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values near rather than below <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. The bluffing and opponent-reading axes depend on LLM judges whose family-aligned bias we mitigate but do not eliminate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panickssery et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The bet-sizing and positional-awareness GTO references are indicative baselines drawn from published solver outputs at similar stack depths, not absolute ground truth at our exact configuration. Model versions drifted over the 27-day collection window, which is a fact of frontier-model evaluation rather than a defect in the design, but it bounds the precision of any version-level claim.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Game-play benchmarks for LLMs.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Games have become the standard testbed for strategic reasoning in LLMs, since each move forces planning under uncertainty against a responsive opponent. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta Fundamental AI Research Diplomacy Team (FAIR) et al. (2022)</a></cite> combined a dialogue model with a search-based planner to reach human-level Diplomacy, and subsequent benchmarks evaluate frozen models on classical games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, social-deduction settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Light et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and task-oriented dialogue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chalamalasetti et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Closer to our setting, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akata et al. (2025)</a></cite> extend this line to repeated <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{\times}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{\times}2</annotation></semantics></math> games with explicit equilibrium analysis, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2025)</a></cite> evaluate strategic planning across a breadth of formal games. Each of these collapses performance to a scalar that obscures the cognitive structure underneath. Multi-axis evaluation suites are now standard outside games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; Poker Arena imports that disaggregation discipline into a payoff-driven adversarial setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Poker AI and imperfect-information games.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Imperfect-information game theory frames poker around two complementary objectives, equilibrium approximation and opponent exploitation. Counterfactual regret minimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zinkevich et al., 2007</a>)</cite> converges in average to a Nash equilibrium and underpinned the weak solution of heads-up limit hold’em <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bowling et al., 2015</a>)</cite>; this equilibrium baseline is what our strategic-mixing axis measures dissociation from. DeepStack, Libratus, and Pluribus carry equilibrium approximation to no-limit and multi-player play through deep value estimation and continual re-solving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moravčík et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown and Sandholm, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown and Sandholm, 2019</a>)</cite>, and reinforcement-learning hybrids extend search to larger imperfect-information settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heinrich and Silver, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. These systems prioritize equilibrium strategies and performance, rather than interpretable cognitive decomposition, and rely on training-time optimization that is not applicable to frozen-weight LLMs. In contrast, our approach reuses poker as an evaluation environment but focuses on extracting interpretable, axis-level diagnostics from gameplay.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Opponent modeling and exploitative play.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equilibrium play is unexploitable but leaves expected value on the table against suboptimal opponents, which is the gap exploitative methods address. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Southey et al. (2005)</a></cite> introduced Bayesian opponent modelling in poker, with posterior beliefs over opponent type driving deviations from equilibrium; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganzfried and Sandholm (2011)</a></cite> formulate opponent modelling in large imperfect-information games as exploitation of deviations from solver play, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganzfried and Sandholm (2015)</a></cite> formalize the trade-off between exploitation gain and bounded loss against unknown opponents. Poker Arena operationalizes exploitation as measurable competence rather than a win-rate residual: we score opponent-read quality in reasoning text, test whether reads translate into observable strategy adjustment, and distinguish deliberate deception from accidental low-equity aggression.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Persistent memory for LLM agents.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Persistent memory matters in long-horizon agent settings. MemGPT pages information between short- and long-term stores under an OS analogy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Packer et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Generative Agents combine a memory stream with reflection and retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and Reflexion shows verbal self-reflection improves sequential decisions without weight updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. None of these systems are evaluated under competitive multi-agent pressure with chip-denominated payoffs. Poker Arena addresses this gap by treating memory as a controllable variable and evaluating its impact through targeted ablations across sessions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theory of mind, deception, and social reasoning.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A final thread studies theory of mind, deception, and social reasoning in LLMs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kosinski (2024)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gandhi et al. (2023)</a></cite> evaluate false-belief reasoning in static settings, and SOTOPIA extends this to interactive social environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Empirical surveys document deceptive behaviour in deployed systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scheurer et al. (2023)</a></cite> show that pressured models can conceal reasoning from a supervisor. These rely on scripted vignettes or self-report; Poker Arena makes the same faculties payoff-bearing inside the game, with theory of mind driving opponent reading, deception measured as intentional bluffing, and social adaptation captured by within- and cross-session adaptability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Extended Results</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section gives the full prose interpretation of each Results subsection in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments and Results ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, expanding the condensed body claims with bootstrap intervals, mechanism narratives, and the model-specific reasoning behind the headline patterns.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Tournament Outcome (extended)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude wins both headline axes by a wide margin.</span> It posts 14 of 50 first-place finishes and a <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+15{,}730" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>15,730</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+15{,}730</annotation></semantics></math>-chip cumulative gain, roughly four times Grok’s <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3{,}705" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3,705</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3{,}705</annotation></semantics></math> second-place margin, on a per-session standard deviation <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Delta}=\$715" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>715</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Delta}=\$715</annotation></semantics></math> that is the largest in the field. The volatility cuts both ways: Claude’s 95% bootstrap interval on cumulative chips (<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6{,}720" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6,720</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6{,}720</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+26{,}351" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>26,351</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+26{,}351</annotation></semantics></math>) is the only positive interval that excludes zero, and Kimi’s (<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-18{,}206" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>18,206</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-18{,}206</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-6{,}693" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6,693</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-6{,}693</annotation></semantics></math>) is the only fully negative interval (Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Extended Results and Statistical Tests ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The middle of the table is statistically a tie.</span> GPT, DeepSeek, Gemini, and Qwen settle within a $2,000 chip-delta band (<math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2{,}785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2,785</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2{,}785</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-937" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>937</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-937</annotation></semantics></math>) with overlapping bootstrap intervals, so a 50-tournament leaderboard cannot rank them with confidence. Kimi sits visibly below the cluster at <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-12{,}558" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12,558</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-12{,}558</annotation></semantics></math> chips and an average finishing position of 5.10 out of 7 (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; per-session deltas in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Per-Model Per-Session Chip Delta ‣ Appendix G Extended Results and Statistical Tests ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>), an outcome the cognitive profile traces back to a small number of specific axes (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Cognitive Profile and Metric Leader ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>b).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Playing Styles (extended)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frontier LLMs converge to qualitatively different table styles, not a single equilibrium</span> (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Cognitive Profile and Metric Leader ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>a). VPIP spans 17.2 percentage points, from Gemini at 14.8% (the tightest profile in the field, folding more than five of every six hands preflop) to GPT at 32.0% (the loosest, entering nearly one in three pots), with the remaining models clustered between 21% and 29%. Aggression factor varies even more dramatically, from Kimi’s 0.69 (passive: roughly two calls for every aggressive action) to Grok’s 3.34 (hyper-aggressive: more than three bets and raises per call). <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Style and outcome do not line up by either axis alone.</span> GPT plays the most hands but finishes mid-pack; Grok bets and raises most often but is second on chips, not first. Claude is the only model that pairs above-median VPIP (28.6%) with above-median AF (2.46), and that joint balance, playing enough hands to apply pressure and following through aggressively when it does, is the simplest one-figure summary of why an evenly competent profile translates into the chip lead.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Cognitive Profile and Metric Leader (extended)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-axis leadership is fragmented across the field</span> (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Cognitive Profile and Metric Leader ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>b). DeepSeek leads bet-sizing calibration (0.79), Grok leads bluffing (0.83) and opponent reading (0.46), GPT leads composure (0.89) and positional awareness (0.83), and Gemini leads factual accuracy (0.74). No model wins more than two axes, and the axes that most separate the field are bluffing, opponent reading, and positional awareness; adaptability and composure cluster within a 0.024 band across all seven models, suggesting a frontier-LLM ceiling on the strategy-adjustment signal a 1,000-hand horizon can resolve.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The mean-axis leader does not win the chip race.</span> Grok tops the aggregate at <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{M}=0.6137" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6137</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{M}=0.6137</annotation></semantics></math> but finishes second on chips, while Claude is fifth on the mean (<math id="A3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{M}=0.5754" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5754</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{M}=0.5754</annotation></semantics></math>) and first on chips. The mechanism is evenness rather than dominance: Claude’s cross-axis standard deviation of 0.143 is the fourth-lowest of the seven models, but its profile has no salient hole, so no single weakness becomes a recurring leak over 1,000 hands. Kimi is the mirror image: bet-sizing of 0.36 and positional awareness of 0.26, the two axes most directly linked to chip-flow discipline, account for most of its <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-12{,}558" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12,558</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-12{,}558</annotation></semantics></math> chip loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The two rankings are positively but loosely correlated.</span> A Spearman test between mean-axis rank and chip rank gives <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{S}=+0.571" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.571</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{S}=+0.571</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.180" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.180</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.180</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>, Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Extended Results and Statistical Tests ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>); the seven-model panel is too small to clear <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, but the sign matches the reading that aggregate competence and chip outcome track each other while neither subsumes the other.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Memory Ablation (extended)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ablation seats Claude, GPT, and Kimi across two conditions paired by game seed for 10 sessions of 10 hands each (600 ablation hands total): condition A loads a prior-tournament summary into Layer 2 at session start, while condition B begins each session with an empty Layer 2 against the same seeds. Table <a href="#A7.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ G.3 Ablation Per-Session Results ‣ Appendix G Extended Results and Statistical Tests ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> reports the paired tests, Figure <a href="#A7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ G.3 Ablation Per-Session Results ‣ Appendix G Extended Results and Statistical Tests ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> visualises the per-condition means with <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, and Appendix <a href="#A7.SS3" title="G.3 Ablation Per-Session Results ‣ Appendix G Extended Results and Statistical Tests ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.3</span></a> lists the per-session deltas.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The three models split into three distinct verdicts.</span> Memory <em id="A3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">helps</em> GPT: it earns <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+130.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>130.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+130.0</annotation></semantics></math> chips per session with seeding and only <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+15.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>15.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+15.4</annotation></semantics></math> without, a <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+114.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>114.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+114.6</annotation></semantics></math> swing (<math id="A3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=+1.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.72</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=+1.72</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.120" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.120</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.120</annotation></semantics></math>); memory <em id="A3.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hurts</em> Kimi: its mean shifts from <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-64.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>64.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-64.3</annotation></semantics></math> with seeding to <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+45.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>45.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+45.1</annotation></semantics></math> without, a <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-109.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>109.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-109.4</annotation></semantics></math> swing (<math id="A3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=-1.84" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.84</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=-1.84</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.099" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.099</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.099</annotation></semantics></math>); memory <em id="A3.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">slightly hurts</em> Claude, moving it from <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-53.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>53.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-53.5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-11.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-11.0</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=-1.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.92</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=-1.92</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.087" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.087</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.087</annotation></semantics></math>). All three <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values sit near <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.10</annotation></semantics></math>, none clear <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, but the directions are clean and the sign of the swing is consistent with non-parametric paired tests on the same data (Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Extended Results and Statistical Tests ‣ Poker Arena: Multi-Axis Profiling of Strategic Reasoning and Memory in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The split is consistent with model-specific use of priors.</span> GPT integrates the seeded summary as an informative prior on opponent style and converts it into chips; Kimi appears to over-anchor on the prior and under-react to the live table, paying for a stale model relative to a fresh slate; Claude is roughly neutral, neither helped nor harmed at this scale, consistent with its even cross-axis profile in the main panel. Whether persistent memory is an asset thus depends on the model’s prior-update behaviour <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Southey et al., 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganzfried and Sandholm, 2011</a>)</cite>, and a benchmark that fixes the memory interface across models will rank them differently than one that varies it.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Full Cognitive Metric Definitions</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix specifies each cognitive axis in full: a prose overview, the formal definition, sub-component weights, any judge prompts or reference tables, a worked numerical example, the corner-case handling, and a one-line reading guide. Subsections share this internal order so a reader who needs only one axis can lift it without reading the rest. The nine metrics aggregate into a single mean as</p>
<table id="A4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{M}=\frac{1}{9}\sum_{k=1}^{9}M_{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>9</mn></munderover><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{M}=\frac{1}{9}\sum_{k=1}^{9}M_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with each <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{k}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{k}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. The metric labelled <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> is named the factual-accuracy module and <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> the positional-awareness module; the labels reflect the consolidated nine-axis inventory used throughout the paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Bet Sizing Calibration</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bet sizing is the most visible surface of a poker strategy, and it is the surface where solver-derived reference distributions are cleanest. The bet-sizing axis aggregates the <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between observed bet-ratio histograms and the corresponding GTO references, so that a model whose sizing matches the solver across spots scores uniformly well, while a model with a single dominant ratio (e.g. always half-pot) takes a per-spot penalty. The metric is computed by the bet-sizing module.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each named action type <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\{\text{cbet},\text{value},\text{3bet},\text{4bet},\text{overbet}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>cbet</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>value</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>3bet</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>4bet</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>overbet</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\{\text{cbet},\text{value},\text{3bet},\text{4bet},\text{overbet}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{7}</annotation></semantics></math> be the observed histogram of bet ratios (amount divided by pot) across seven size buckets and let <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the GTO reference distribution. The per-type score is</p>
<table id="A4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}=1-\frac{\lVert\hat{p}_{t}-p_{t}^{*}\rVert_{1}}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}=1-\frac{\lVert\hat{p}_{t}-p_{t}^{*}\rVert_{1}}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the factor of two normalises the maximum possible <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between two probability vectors. Types with fewer than <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\min}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\min}=10</annotation></semantics></math> observations are excluded. The preflop sub-score averages <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{S_{3\text{bet}},S_{4\text{bet}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>bet</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>bet</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{3\text{bet}},S_{4\text{bet}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the postflop sub-score averages <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{S_{\text{cbet}},S_{\text{value}},S_{\text{overbet}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>cbet</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>value</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>overbet</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{\text{cbet}},S_{\text{value}},S_{\text{overbet}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{pre}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{pre}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{post}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>post</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{post}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the counts of aggressive actions on each street, we set <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\mathrm{post}}=n_{\mathrm{post}}/(n_{\mathrm{pre}}+n_{\mathrm{post}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>post</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>post</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>post</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\mathrm{post}}=n_{\mathrm{post}}/(n_{\mathrm{pre}}+n_{\mathrm{post}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\mathrm{pre}}=1-w_{\mathrm{post}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>post</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\mathrm{pre}}=1-w_{\mathrm{post}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
<table id="A4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{M1}=w_{\mathrm{post}}\cdot S_{\mathrm{post}}+w_{\mathrm{pre}}\cdot S_{\mathrm{pre}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>M1</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>post</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>post</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{M1}=w_{\mathrm{post}}\cdot S_{\mathrm{post}}+w_{\mathrm{pre}}\cdot S_{\mathrm{pre}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">When <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{post}}&lt;20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>post</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{post}}&lt;20</annotation></semantics></math> the postflop score is omitted, only <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{pre}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pre</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{pre}}</annotation></semantics></math> is returned, and a low-confidence flag is raised.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The bucket boundaries, expressed as fractions of the pot, are <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,0.25)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,0.25)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.25,0.40)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.40</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.25,0.40)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.40,0.60)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.40</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.60</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.40,0.60)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.60,0.80)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.60</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.80</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.60,0.80)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.80,1.10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.80</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.80,1.10)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1.10,1.50)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1.10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.50</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1.10,1.50)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1.50,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1.50</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1.50,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>. Reference weights <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> come from a shared GTO reference table:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Type</span></th>
<th id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0–25</span></th>
<th id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">25–40</span></th>
<th id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">40–60</span></th>
<th id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">60–80</span></th>
<th id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">80–110</span></th>
<th id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">110–150</span></th>
